{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Plenty of real problems hand you **more than one** array: two sorted lists of user IDs to combine, today's and yesterday's prices to compare, three teams' schedules to find a common free slot. This chapter covers the most asked problems of that kind."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Almost all of them are solved by one idea: **walk the arrays together**, with one pointer per array, and decide at each step which pointer to move."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/5fccf9a5-71f5-451e-bf6e-3477418761e4.svg",
      "alt": "Two sorted arrays a = [1, 3, 5, 7] and b = [2, 4, 6], with pointer i on a's first element and pointer j on b's first element. The rule: compare the two front values and move the pointer on the smaller one.",
      "width": 832,
      "height": 467,
      "scene": {
