    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why sorted inputs make this fast"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If both arrays are **sorted**, the smallest value you haven't used yet is always sitting at the **front** of one of them — at `a[i]` or at `b[j]`. So one comparison tells you what comes next, and each pointer only ever moves forward. Every element is looked at once: **O(n + m)** instead of comparing every pair in **O(n × m)**."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If the arrays are **not** sorted, you have two options: sort them first (O(n log n)) and walk them together, or put one array into a **hash set** and look up the other's values in O(1) each. Both appear in this chapter."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The problems in this chapter"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "**Merge two sorted arrays** — the purest form of walking two arrays together.",
        "**Merge sorted arrays in place** — the same, but written into the first array, which means filling it from the **back**.",
        "**Intersection of two arrays** — values present in both; sorting first, or using a set.",
        "**Union of two sorted arrays** — every distinct value from either array.",
        "**Common elements in three sorted arrays** — the idea stretched to three pointers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Three questions to ask every time"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Are the inputs sorted?** If yes, walking them together is almost always the answer. If not, is it cheaper to sort or to hash?",
        "**Which pointer moves?** Usually the one pointing at the smaller value — it can no longer match anything ahead in the other array.",
        "**What happens when one array runs out?** The leftovers of the other array often still need handling — copying them over, or stopping early."
      ]
    },
